# source = Plato, Crito_45
# text = ἔτι δέ , ὦ Σώκρατες , οὐ δὲ δίκαιόν μοι δοκεῖς ἐπιχειρεῖν πρᾶγμα , σαυτὸν προδοῦναι , ἐξὸν σωθῆναι , καὶ τοιαῦτα σπεύδεις περὶ σαυτὸν γενέσθαι ἅπερ ἂν καὶ οἱ ἐχθροί σου σπεύσαιέν τε καὶ ἔσπευσαν σὲ διαφθεῖραι βουλόμενοι .
# sent_id = 97
# subdoc = 45c.2
# literal_translation = And further, O Socrates, you do not seem to me to be attempting a just thing, to betray yourself, when it is possible to be saved, and you are eager that such things happen to yourself as your enemies would be eager and have been eager to do, wanting to destroy you.
# readable_translation = And further, Socrates, I don't think you're doing the right thing in trying to betray yourself, when it's possible to be saved, and you're eager to bring upon yourself just what your enemies would be eager—and have been eager—to do, wanting to destroy you.
