# source = Plato, Crito_47
# text = οὐκοῦν καὶ τ ἆλλα , ὦ Κρίτων , οὕτως , ἵνα μὴ πάντα διΐωμεν , καὶ δὴ καὶ περὶ τῶν δικαίων καὶ ἀδίκων καὶ αἰσχρῶν καὶ καλῶν καὶ ἀγαθῶν καὶ κακῶν , περὶ ὧν νῦν ἡ βουλὴ ἡμῖν ἐστιν , πότερον τῇ τῶν πολλῶν δόξῃ δεῖ ἡμᾶς ἕπεσθαι καὶ φοβεῖσθαι αὐτὴν ἢ τῇ τοῦ ἑνός , εἴ τίς ἐστιν ἐπαΐων , ὃν δεῖ καὶ αἰσχύνεσθαι καὶ φοβεῖσθαι μᾶλλον ἢ σύμπαντας τοὺς ἄλλους [0] [1] [2] ;
# sent_id = 162
# subdoc = 47d.1
# literal_translation = Therefore also the other things, O Crito, thus, in order that we may not go through all things, and indeed also concerning the just and unjust and shameful and noble and good and bad, concerning which now the deliberation for us is, whether to the opinion of the many we must follow and fear it, or to that of the one, if there is any expert, whom we must both respect and fear more than all the others [we must follow and fear]?
# readable_translation = Then also the other things, Crito, are like this, so that we need not go through them all—and in particular concerning the just and unjust, the shameful and noble, the good and bad, about which our present deliberation is: should we follow and fear the opinion of the many, or that of the one person, if there is an expert, whom we should respect and fear more than all the others?
