# source = Plato, Crito_53
# text = αὐτὸς δὲ πρῶτον μὲν ἐὰν εἰς τῶν ἐγγύτατά τινα πόλεων ἔλθῃς , ἢ Θήβαζε ἢ Μέγαράδε — εὐνομοῦνται γὰρ ἀμφότεραι — πολέμιος ἥξεις , ὦ Σώκρατες , τῇ τούτων πολιτείᾳ , καὶ ὅσοιπερ κήδονται τῶν αὑτῶν πόλεων ὑποβλέψονταί σε διαφθορέα ἡγούμενοι τῶν νόμων , καὶ βεβαιώσεις τοῖς δικασταῖς τὴν δόξαν , ὥστε δοκεῖν ὀρθῶς τὴν δίκην δικάσαι ·
# sent_id = 339
# subdoc = 53b.3
# literal_translation = But you yourself, first, if you go to one of the nearest cities, either to Thebes or to Megara — for both are well-governed — you will come as an enemy, O Socrates, to their constitution, and all who care for their own cities will look askance at you, considering you a corrupter of the laws, and you will confirm the opinion of the judges, so that they seem to have judged the case rightly.
# readable_translation = But you yourself, first, if you go to one of the nearest cities, either Thebes or Megara — for both are well-governed — you will come as an enemy, Socrates, to their constitution, and all who care for their own cities will look askance at you, considering you a corrupter of the laws, and you will confirm the judges' opinion, so that they will seem to have judged the case rightly.
